From my side, I am mainly looking at this through the lens of Cyber and PI (including Tech E&O), where AI governance is becoming an increasingly important topic for underwriting and AI customers. I am hence interested in how you are approaching this as Governance as a Service.

 

    My understanding is that the tool could support areas such as risk prevention, pre-bind underwriting assessment, ongoing risk management and potentially claims support, particularly for AI-core insureds where AI is the main product. We tend to focus on SMEs (companies with turnover up to €1b) , so while SME AI customers volume may not yet be significant, I do expect this space to grow over time. I would also be interested to understand how the tool could apply where AI is embedded into the workflow of a SaaS company, or where AI is used internally within an organisation. In those cases, how could the tool help CISOs identify governance or control gaps and translate those into practical risk improvements?

 

    I would also be keen to understand how your continuous monitoring and telemetry work in practice. In cyber, we already see similar concepts through vulnerability scans and scoring, which can be useful but are highly dependent on the timing of the scan, the methodology used, and the risk of false positives or false negatives. It would therefore be helpful to understand what sits “under the hood”, how evidence is generated, and whether the tool provides corrective measures or active guidance where issues are identified.

 

    More broadly, the idea of AI governing AI is very relevant, and probably unavoidable at scale. At the same time, I would be interested in your view on where human oversight remains necessary, and how your model helps distinguish between autonomous AI error, human approval or override, vendor/tool failure, control failure and cyber compromise.

 

